**Quick answer:** To tailor your resume, keep one complete master resume, then for each job make a copy and change only what matters: reorder bullets so the most relevant come first, rewrite them in the posting's vocabulary where it's true, add real experience you left out, and remove what doesn't help. Never add skills or numbers you can't defend. On GetHired, the agent compares your resume with a job and suggests changes you tick, edit or skip before anything is saved.

A tailored resume isn't a different story for every job. It's the same true story with the relevant parts easy to find.

## What should you change when tailoring a resume?

{% table %}
-
- Change it
- Leave it alone
---
- Order of bullets within each role
- Job titles you held
---
- Wording that describes the same work in the employer's terms
- Employment dates
---
- Your summary
- Companies and degrees
---
- Which projects and skills you show
- Numbers you measured
{% /table %}

## How do you tailor a resume in 15 minutes?

### 1. Pull out the real requirements

Read the posting and write down the three to five things the role depends on. They're usually repeated, listed first, or marked as required.

### 2. Match each requirement to your experience

For each one, find the bullet that proves it. If you have the experience but it isn't written down, that's your most valuable edit. If you don't have it, leave it out.

### 3. Move the proof to the top

Put the bullets that match first under each role. A reader scanning quickly sees the top of each section.

### 4. Rewrite in their vocabulary, truthfully

If the posting says "CI/CD pipelines" and you wrote "automated our releases", use their term if it describes what you did. See our [30 resume bullet examples](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-write-resume-bullet-points-that-get-interviews-30-examples-for-tech-roles) for structure.

### 5. Cut what doesn't help

Removing three irrelevant bullets often does more than adding one.

## How do you tailor a resume with AI on GetHired?

1. **Add your resume.** Upload a PDF or Word (.docx) file up to 2 MB, or start from a blank one. GetHired turns it into an editable resume and doesn't keep the original file, so check it over in the editor. Uploading uses AI credits.
2. **Ask the agent.** Open the [agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/agent) and say "Improve my resume for this job", then point it at the posting.
3. **Review the changes card.** Suggestions can rewrite a bullet, add one, remove one or move one. Tick the ones you want, edit the wording, and preview the whole resume.
4. **Apply.** By default your changes are saved to a new copy, so the original stays as it is. You can choose to change the original instead.

Nothing is applied until you click, and applied changes can be undone. Improving a resume uses AI credits, shown in your account.

## What if the AI needs a number you haven't given?

It shouldn't guess. If a metric would strengthen a bullet and your resume doesn't have one, the suggestion contains a placeholder such as `[X%]` or `[number of users]`. You can't apply it until you fill in the real value or leave that change out.

Only enter a number you could explain in an interview. "How did you measure that?" is a very common follow-up question. If you don't have a figure, describe the result in words instead: "cut a manual weekly report down to an automated one" is honest and still specific.

## How do you manage several versions of your resume?

- **Keep a master.** It holds everything, and it can be longer than anything you send.
- **Copy before you tailor.** On GetHired, **Make a copy** keeps the original untouched.
- **Name copies by role or company,** so you can find them later. Copies made while applying suggestions are named for you.
- **Set one as primary.** Your primary resume is the one GetHired uses to match jobs to you.

You can keep up to 50 resumes. More on this in our [multi-resume strategy guide](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/mastering-the-multi-resume-strategy-tailoring-your-technical-identity-with-get-hired-ai).

When you apply through GetHired, the company sees the resume as it was when you sent it. Editing your resume later doesn't change what they received.

## Can you export a tailored resume?

Yes. Download any resume as a **PDF** or **Word** document. There are two templates, **Classic** and **Compact**. Both use a single column, standard headings and real text, without tables or graphics, which helps applicant tracking systems read them. You can [check your resume against ATS basics](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-check-if-your-resume-will-pass-an-ats-in-2026-and-fix-it-in-5-minutes) too.

If your resume uses characters the PDF fonts don't cover, such as some Asian scripts or emoji, download the Word version.

## Frequently asked questions

### Should I tailor my resume for every job?

Tailor it for jobs you really want. For very similar roles, one tailored copy per role type is usually enough.

### Is it dishonest to tailor a resume?

No. Choosing what to emphasise is normal. Adding experience, skills or results you don't have is dishonest, and interviews tend to expose it.

### Should I copy keywords from the job description?

Use the posting's terms where they accurately describe your work, inside real sentences. Don't paste a list of keywords or hide text.

### Does GetHired change my resume automatically?

No. The agent suggests changes, and you choose which to apply. By default they go into a copy.

### How long should tailoring take?

About 15 minutes by hand once you have a strong master resume. Suggestions can shorten that, but always read each change before applying it.

